tel 1.0

base monotonous
import soundness

info   base encodings of bytes as text: Base64, Base32, hexadecimal, octal, quaternary and binary
detail
    _Monotonous_ serializes bytes to text, and deserializes text back to bytes, in the
    binary-to-text encodings: Base64, Base32, hexadecimal (`Hex`), `Octal`, `Quaternary` and
    `Binary`. The encoding is named as a type, and the exact character set — the `Alphabet` —
    is a given chosen by importing it by name from the `alphabets` package, so the standard and
    URL-safe forms of Base64 are a one-line difference.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import alphabets.base64Standard
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val bytes: Data = t"Hello".in[Data]
    val text: Text = bytes.serialize[Base64]   // t"SGVsbG8="
    val back: Data = text.deserialize[Base64]
    ```

    This is *serialization*, not encoding in the sense of JSON or XML: bytes become text with
    no regard for what the bytes mean, which is why the operations are `serialize` and
    `deserialize`. `serialize` takes any value which is `Encodable in Data`; `deserialize`
    validates every character against the alphabet and raises a `Serialization.Error` for one
    it does not contain, so it needs a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`.

    No alphabet is in scope by default: the `alphabets` package's givens must be imported by
    name, since `import alphabets.*` does not import givens, and `import alphabets.given`
    brings in every alphabet of each encoding at once, which is ambiguous. A *strict* alphabet
    reads back only its own characters, while a *tolerant* one also accepts recognised
    equivalents (the other case of hexadecimal, say), so the strictness of a decode is chosen
    by the import.

    An `Alphabet` is also a stream stage: a `Stream[Data]` serializes as it flows, and a
    `Stream[Text]` deserializes, with the same results as the whole-value forms whatever the
    chunking.

    The library is the single source set `monotonous.core`, which compiles for the JVM,
    Scala.js and Scala Native. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and its
    `alphabets` package merges with the umbrella's own.

refer monotonous.serialize
refer monotonous.deserialize
refer monotonous.Alphabet
refer monotonous.alphabets

entry .serialize   renders bytes, or a stream of bytes, as text in a base encoding
    `value.serialize[Base64]` takes any value which is `Encodable in Data` — `Data` itself, or
    any type with an instance — and renders its bytes with the `Serializable in Base64` given,
    which draws its characters from the `Alphabet[Base64]` in scope. The encoding is the type
    argument: `Base64`, `Base32`, `Hex`, `Octal`, `Quaternary` or `Binary`.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label The standard Base64 alphabet, padded with `=`
      code
          locally:
            import alphabets.base64Standard
            Data(-5, -1).serialize[Base64]   // t"+/8="
    frame
      label The URL-safe alphabet: `-` and `_` for `+` and `/`, and no padding
      code
          locally:
            import alphabets.base64Url
            Data(-5, -1).serialize[Base64]   // t"-_8"
    frame
      label Lower-case hexadecimal
      code
          locally:
            import alphabets.hexLowerCase
            Data(-5, -1).serialize[Hex]      // t"fbff"
    frame
      label Binary
      code
          locally:
            import alphabets.binaryStandard
            Data(-5, -1).serialize[Binary]   // t"1111101111111111"
    ##
    ```

    An alphabet with padding pads the output to a whole group (four characters for Base64,
    eight for Base32); one without stops at the last character that carries data.

    The same name is an extension on a `Stream[Data] over Credit`, which serializes the stream
    as it flows into a `Stream[Text] over Credit`, using the `Alphabet` in scope as a stream
    stage and a `Buffering` (there is a standard one in its companion). Groups which straddle
    two chunks are carried across, so the streamed text equals the whole-value form.

entry .deserialize   reads text, or a stream of text, back to bytes in a base encoding
    `text.deserialize[Base64]` reads `Text` back to `Data` with the `Deserializable in Base64`
    given, which draws on the `Alphabet[Base64]` in scope. Each character is checked against
    the alphabet, including its tolerated alternatives, and the first which is not in it is
    raised as a `Serialization.Error` with its position; trailing padding is ignored. It needs
    a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A tolerant alphabet accepts either case
      code
          locally:
            import alphabets.hexLowerCase
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            t"FBFF".deserialize[Hex]   // the two bytes 0xfb, 0xff
    frame
      label A strict alphabet accepts only its own characters
      code
          locally:
            import alphabets.hexStrictLowerCase
            capture[Serialization.Error](t"FBFF".deserialize[Hex])
            // Serialization.Error(0, 'F')
    ##
    ```

    The same name is an extension on a `Stream[Text] over Credit`, which deserializes as the
    text flows, into a `Stream[Data] over Credit`. It needs the `Alphabet` in scope, a
    `Buffering`, and a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`; incomplete groups and padding split
    across chunks are carried over, so any chunking gives the bytes the whole-value form does.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import alphabets.base64Standard
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val chunks: Stream[Text] over Credit = Stream(t"SGVsbG8=")
    chunks.deserialize[Base64]
    ```

entry .Serialization   the supertype of the encodings, and the home of `Serialization.Error`
  detail
      Each encoding is a sealed subtrait of `Serialization` — `Base64`, `Base32`, `Hex`,
      `Octal`, `Quaternary` and `Binary` — used only as a type argument, to `serialize[Base64]`
      or `Alphabet[Hex]`, and never instantiated.
  refer monotonous.Serialization.Error
  entry .Error   raised when text to be deserialized holds a character not in the alphabet
    detail
        Error 325: _the character `char` at `position` was not valid_. Raised by `deserialize`
        and by the streaming deserializer when a character is neither one of the alphabet's
        own nor one of its tolerated alternatives. A strict alphabet raises it for the other
        case of a letter, where a tolerant one would accept it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import alphabets.base32LowerCase

        capture[Serialization.Error](t"AAAQEA4?DQKAYB7H5737Q====".deserialize[Base32])
        // Serialization.Error(7, '?')
        ```
    entry #position   the index, from zero, of the invalid character in the text
    entry #char       the invalid character

entry .Base64   the Base64 encoding: six bits per character, in groups of four for three bytes
    A type argument for `serialize`, `deserialize` and `Alphabet`, never instantiated. Its
    alphabets in the `alphabets` package are `base64Standard` (RFC 4648, padded),
    `base64Unpadded`, `base64Url`, `base64Xml`, `base64Imap`, `base64Yui`, `base64Radix64`,
    `base64Bcrypt`, `base64Sasl` and `base64Uuencoding`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import alphabets.base64Standard

    Data(0, 1, 2, 3).serialize[Base64]   // t"AAECAw=="
    ```

entry .Base32   the Base32 encoding: five bits per character, in groups of eight for five bytes
    A type argument for `serialize`, `deserialize` and `Alphabet`, never instantiated. Its
    alphabets in the `alphabets` package include RFC 4648's in strict and tolerant upper and
    lower case (`base32UpperCase`, `base32StrictUpperCase`, …), the extended-hex forms,
    z-base-32, Geohash, word-safe and Crockford's.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import alphabets.base32UpperCase

    Data(0, 1, 2, 3).serialize[Base32]   // t"AAAQEAY="
    ```

entry .Hex   hexadecimal: four bits per character, two characters for each byte
  detail
      A type argument for `serialize`, `deserialize` and `Alphabet`, never instantiated. Its
      alphabets in the `alphabets` package are `hexUpperCase` and `hexLowerCase`, which read
      either case, the strict `hexStrictUpperCase` and `hexStrictLowerCase`, and `hexBioctal`.
      Hexadecimal is never padded.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import alphabets.hexUpperCase

      Data(-5, -1).serialize[Hex]   // t"FBFF"
      ```

      The `Hex` object is also an extractor for a hexadecimal number written as text (`.unapply`).
  entry .unapply   matches text which is a hexadecimal number, giving its value as an `Int`
      `case Hex(n)` matches text which `Integer.parseInt(text, 16)` reads — digits of either
      case, with an optional sign — and binds its value; any other text, or a number too
      large for an `Int`, does not match.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      t"ff" match
        case Hex(value) => value   // 255
        case _          => -1
      ```

entry .Octal   octal: three bits per character
    A type argument for `serialize`, `deserialize` and `Alphabet`, never instantiated. Its one
    alphabet is `alphabets.octalStandard`, the digits `0` to `7`, unpadded; since three bits
    do not divide a byte, the last character may hold fewer bits of data.

entry .Quaternary   base four: two bits per character, four characters for each byte
    A type argument for `serialize`, `deserialize` and `Alphabet`, never instantiated. Its
    alphabets are `alphabets.quaternaryStandard` (`0123`) and
    `alphabets.quaternaryDnaNucleotide` (`ATCG`).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import alphabets.quaternaryDnaNucleotide

    Data(27).serialize[Quaternary]   // t"ATCG"
    ```

entry .Binary   binary: one bit per character, eight characters for each byte
    A type argument for `serialize`, `deserialize` and `Alphabet`, never instantiated. Its
    one alphabet is `alphabets.binaryStandard`, the digits `0` and `1`.

entry .Alphabet   the characters a base encoding uses, its padding, and the alternatives it tolerates
  detail
      An `Alphabet[encoding]` holds the `#chars` of the encoding in order of value — two for
      `Binary`, sixteen for `Hex`, sixty-four for `Base64` — followed, if `#padding` is set,
      by the padding character. Its `#tolerance` maps further characters to values, which
      deserialization also accepts: this is what makes `hexLowerCase` read upper-case digits,
      and `base32Crockford` read `O` as `0` and `I` and `L` as `1`.

      The standard alphabets are givens in the `alphabets` package, imported by name. A custom
      one is a local given:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Alphabet[Hex] = Alphabet(t"0123456789ABCDEF", padding = false)
      Data(-5, -1).serialize[Hex]   // t"FBFF"
      ```

      The text must have exactly `2^bits` characters for the encoding, plus one for padding
      when `padding` is `true`; nothing checks this, and a wrong length gives wrong output.
      An alphabet is pure (`caps.Pure`), so it may not capture a capability.

      An alphabet is also a stream stage, through the companion's `.serialization` and
      `.deserialization` givens: `stream.via(alphabet)` serializes a `Stream[Data]` or
      deserializes a `Stream[Text]`, chosen by the stream's element type. The `serialize` and
      `deserialize` extensions on streams are this, with the alphabet summoned.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      Data(-5, -1).stream.via(summon[Alphabet[Hex]])
      ```
  refer monotonous.alphabets
  refer monotonous.serialize
  entry .serialization     streams bytes through an alphabet as a stage, giving text
      The `Ductile` given which lets `via(alphabet)` serialize a `Stream[Data] over Credit`
      into a `Stream[Text] over Credit`. Demand is translated by the exact arithmetic of the
      base, so a downstream request for 1024 hexadecimal characters asks upstream for 512
      bytes; padding, if the alphabet has it, is written when the stream ends.
  entry .deserialization   streams text through an alphabet as a stage, giving bytes
      The `Ductile` given which lets `via(alphabet)` deserialize a `Stream[Text] over Credit`
      into a `Stream[Data] over Credit`. It needs a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`, raised for
      a character not in the alphabet, and the instance captures that tactic. Groups and
      padding split between chunks are carried across.
  entry #chars        the encoding's characters in order of value, then the padding character if any
  entry #padding      whether output is padded to a whole group with the last character of `#chars`
  entry #tolerance    further characters accepted when deserializing, mapped to the values they stand for
      Empty for a strict alphabet. For a tolerant one, usually the inverse of the strict
      alphabet of the other case, as `hexUpperCase`'s is `hexStrictLowerCase.inverse`.
  entry #apply        the character for a value, from zero
  entry #invert       the value of a character, or a `Serialization.Error` at the given position
      `alphabet.invert(position, char)` looks the character up in `#inversions`; one with no
      value raises `Serialization.Error(position, char)`, so it needs a
      `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`.
  entry #inverse      every character the alphabet accepts, mapped to its value
      The alphabet's own characters (with the padding character, if any, as the value after
      the last) and its `#tolerance`, as one `Map`.
  entry #inversions   `#inverse` as a dense table indexed by character code, with `-1` for no value
      Computed once, lazily, so that deserialization reads an array rather than a `Map` for
      each character.

entry .Serializable   renders bytes as text in one encoding: the typeclass behind `serialize`
  detail
      A `Serializable in Base64` turns `Data` into `Text` with `#encode`. It has no `Self`
      type, and is selected by its `#Form` alone. The companion has one given for each
      encoding, each built from the `Alphabet` of that encoding in scope, so an instance
      exists exactly when an alphabet has been imported. The kernels for Base64, Base32 and
      hexadecimal are specialised, and all write ASCII bytes directly, so a serialized `Text`
      is built without a character-by-character copy.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import alphabets.base64Standard

      summon[Serializable in Base64].encode(Data(0, 1, 2, 3))   // t"AAECAw=="
      ```

      A `Serializable` is `Findable`, so a compile error for a missing one can propose the
      alphabet imports which would supply it.
  refer monotonous.serialize
  refer monotonous.Deserializable
  entry .base         a `Serializable` for an encoding of the given bits per character, over the `Alphabet` in scope
      `Serializable.base[Base64](6)`, the constructor behind the companion's givens, which a
      new `Serialization` subtype could use with its own alphabet.
  entry .binary       serializes `Binary`, with the `Alphabet[Binary]` in scope
  entry .quaternary   serializes `Quaternary`, with the `Alphabet[Quaternary]` in scope
  entry .octal        serializes `Octal`, with the `Alphabet[Octal]` in scope
  entry .hex          serializes `Hex`, with the `Alphabet[Hex]` in scope
  entry .base32       serializes `Base32`, with the `Alphabet[Base32]` in scope
  entry .base64       serializes `Base64`, with the `Alphabet[Base64]` in scope
  entry #Form         the encoding this instance serializes to
  entry #encode       the bytes, rendered as text in the encoding

entry .Deserializable   reads text back to bytes in one encoding: the typeclass behind `deserialize`
  detail
      A `Deserializable in Base64` turns `Text` into `Data` with `#deserialize`, raising a
      `Serialization.Error` for a character not in the alphabet. It has no `Self` type, and is
      selected by its `#Form` alone. The companion has one given for each encoding, each built
      from the `Alphabet` of that encoding in scope. Error handling is per call, so the
      instances capture nothing.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import alphabets.base64Standard
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      summon[Deserializable in Base64].deserialize(t"AAECAw==")
      ```

      A `Deserializable` is `Findable`, so a compile error for a missing one can propose the
      alphabet imports which would supply it.
  refer monotonous.deserialize
  refer monotonous.Serializable
  entry .base         a `Deserializable` for an encoding of the given bits per character, over the `Alphabet` in scope
  entry .base64       deserializes `Base64`, with the `Alphabet[Base64]` in scope
  entry .base32       deserializes `Base32`, with the `Alphabet[Base32]` in scope
  entry .hex          deserializes `Hex`, with the `Alphabet[Hex]` in scope
  entry .octal        deserializes `Octal`, with the `Alphabet[Octal]` in scope
  entry .quaternary   deserializes `Quaternary`, with the `Alphabet[Quaternary]` in scope
  entry .binary       deserializes `Binary`, with the `Alphabet[Binary]` in scope
  entry #Form         the encoding this instance deserializes from
  entry #deserialize   reads text in the encoding back to bytes, raising `Serialization.Error`
      `deserialize(text)` reads the whole text, needing a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`. The
      four-parameter form, `deserialize(previous, current, index, last)`, decodes one chunk
      of a longer text, with the end of the previous chunk available for a group which
      straddles the two, and `last` set for the final chunk, whose trailing padding is
      dropped; it is the building block for incremental decoding.

entry .alphabets   the standard alphabets of every encoding, each a given to import by name
  detail
      The alphabet in scope decides the exact characters of `serialize` and `deserialize`, and
      none is in scope by default: one is imported by name, since `import alphabets.*` does not
      import givens. Importing two alphabets of the same encoding is ambiguous.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import alphabets.base64Url
      ```

      For most encodings there is a *strict* alphabet, which deserializes only its own
      characters, and a *tolerant* one, which also accepts the other case: `hexLowerCase`
      writes lower case and reads either, where `hexStrictLowerCase` reads only lower case.
      The tolerant ones are named without `Strict`, as they are the usual choice.
  refer monotonous.Alphabet
  entry .binaryStandard               binary: `0` and `1`
  entry .quaternaryStandard           base four: `0123`
  entry .quaternaryDnaNucleotide      base four as DNA nucleotides: `ATCG`
  entry .octalStandard                octal: the digits `0` to `7`, unpadded
  entry .hexStrictUpperCase           hexadecimal in upper case, reading upper case only
  entry .hexStrictLowerCase           hexadecimal in lower case, reading lower case only
  entry .hexUpperCase                 hexadecimal in upper case, reading either case
  entry .hexLowerCase                 hexadecimal in lower case, reading either case
  entry .hexBioctal                   "bioctal" hexadecimal, with the digits `01234567` and the letters `cjzwfsbv`
  entry .base32StrictUpperCase        RFC 4648 Base32 in upper case, padded, reading upper case only
  entry .base32StrictLowerCase        RFC 4648 Base32 in lower case, padded, reading lower case only
  entry .base32UpperCase              RFC 4648 Base32 in upper case, padded, reading either case
  entry .base32LowerCase              RFC 4648 Base32 in lower case, padded, reading either case
  entry .base32ExtendedHexUpperCase   RFC 4648 "base32hex" (`0`–`9`, `A`–`V`) in upper case, padded
      Writes upper case. Its tolerance is the lower-case standard Base32 alphabet's, not the
      lower-case extended-hex one.
  entry .base32ExtendedHexLowerCase   RFC 4648 "base32hex" (`0`–`9`, `a`–`v`) in lower case, padded
      Writes lower case. Its tolerance is the upper-case standard Base32 alphabet's, not the
      upper-case extended-hex one.
  entry .base32ZBase32                z-base-32, the human-oriented ordering, padded
  entry .base32ZBase32Unpadded        z-base-32, unpadded
  entry .base32Geohash                the Geohash alphabet, unpadded
  entry .base32WordSafe               the word-safe alphabet, which avoids spelling words, unpadded
  entry .base32Crockford              Crockford's Base32, unpadded, reading `O` as `0`, `I` and `L` as `1`, and either case
  entry .base64Standard               RFC 4648 Base64, with `+` and `/`, padded with `=`
  entry .base64Unpadded               RFC 4648 Base64, with `+` and `/`, unpadded
  entry .base64Url                    URL- and filename-safe Base64, with `-` and `_`, unpadded
  entry .base64Xml                    Base64 for XML names, with `+` and `-`, padded with `.`
  entry .base64Imap                   modified Base64 of IMAP mailbox names, with `+` and `,`, unpadded
  entry .base64Yui                    the YUI library's Base64, with `.` and `_`, unpadded
  entry .base64Radix64                OpenPGP's Radix-64, the standard alphabet, padded with `=`
  entry .base64Bcrypt                 bcrypt's Base64, with `.` and `/` first, unpadded
  entry .base64Sasl                   SASL's Base64, with `+` and `,`, unpadded
  entry .base64Uuencoding             the uuencoding characters, from `!` to `_`, unpadded
